Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public.

Bonus Lesson #3:
Questions & Answers + Rebuttals to Overcome Objections and Close the
Client

Hey there Bruce! You've made it through 2/3 of the course. We've
covered the basics of the FDCPA so that you're equipped with handling
collection debt, expired debt and all of those sneaky little collection
agencies and junk debt buyers. We've also went over the basics of the
FCRA to gain an understanding of what is required of the consumer
reporting agencies and data furnishers during the verification process.
This means you have the tools necessary for Debt Verification and
Validation, the basics of the Dispute Process. The remaining part of the
course will cover dispute flows when dealing with the consumer reporting
agencies, creditors, and collectors. But before we get into all of that,
let's take a break with come bonus content. In the coming days I advise
that you take the time to go back over the lessons and your notes to
ensure that you have an understanding of the material we've covered in
Dispute 21 so far. Understanding the information you've been presented
is important to carrying out the dispute flow. As the business owner,
it's not just enough to know the what, you will need to know the why and
how in order to be successful in this part of your business. 

Now, on to today's content... 

Bruce, Customer objections can be difficult to handle, and many
sales professionals fear this situation (Yes, I know that you are in
credit repair. But, like it or not, as a business owner you ARE a sales
professional. Or at least you'd want to become one if you want to stay
in business). However, objections communicate important and valuable
information about customer concerns, needs, and fears. Most objections
can be effectively handled with a simple re-framing process that puts
the issue into perspective.

Customer objections can revolve around an array of issues. They may have
concerns about the sincerity of the salesperson, the quality of the
product, the reliability of service, the price, or the amount of risk
involved. 

Four Types of Objections 

Objections can be generally classified into four types:

1. Price/Risk

Price, cost, budget, or ROI concerns all fall into this category. Price
objections are often really about risk. If the sales representative has
justified the cost by building value during the interaction, the
customer will be less worried.

2. Quality of Service

If the customer is concerned about the quality of your products or
services - e.g., they express doubts about product quality, the
training of your personnel, speed or responsiveness of service, or
compatibility - these are examples of quality of service concerns.

3. Trust/Relationship

The customer might be concerned with the legitimacy or credibility of
you or your company. (This indicates that a good relationship has not
been established between the salesperson and the client.)

4. Stall

Customers sometimes attempt to stall their decision. The closer the sale
is to closing, the more pressure the customer feels, and if there is any
remaining conflict or anxiety (or no sense of urgency) they may try to
stall.

If you have anticipated and prepared for the four types of objections,
you won't panic when an objection comes up. Remember that the customer
is behaving quite normally for anyone about to take a risk and make a
purchase. The customer is uncertain about making a change, and needs
reassurance. 

The best course of action is to direct their focus back to the larger
context in which the purchase is being made. Below are some rebuttals
and answers to what we've found to be the most commonly asked credit
repair questions.

Rebuttals & Answers

**To the most common objections and frequently asked questions regarding
credit repair services** 

**#1- "l can't afford it"**
I know times are tough, but you cannot afford not to do this! Think
about it for just a minute. Everything you do these days is based on
your credit scores. Your interest rates, your home, your vehicles,
building a business, insurance and in many cases even your employers
determine eligibility based on your credit. By spending a little time
and money now, we will save you tens or even hundreds of thousands of
dollars in the future. I'm sure you can find a way to make this work.
I've already given you the lowest possible price, I can push your
payment out two weeks - That will work for you right?

**#2- "I need to think about it"**
With all due respect, what is there to think about? You need this, more
than anything to reach your goals. Let's do this!

**#3- "I need to talk to my spouse first"**
I understand and I don't blame you. Is he/she available to talk now? I
can conference him/her in. Our agreement has a 3 day right to cancel. If
you sign up now and your husband/wife does not agree with it; you have
the option of cancelling. Let's get the ball rolling and I would be
more than happy to explain everything to him/her when he/she is
available.

**#4- "I can do this myself"**
Sure, you can try. You can also give yourself a root canal. Look, we are
the experts and it's not just a convenience. The processes we do are
tedious and frustrating, at best. By hiring us, you can sit back, relax
and rest assured that you are going to get the best results possible.
Let's get started!

#5- "Other companies charge less"
Most repair companies actually charge more and do less than what we do.
If you are looking for a basic, long term program; go with someone else.
If you want a comprehensive and effective program, look no further!
Let's do this!

**#6- "What if I've been through bankruptcy?"**
We have had success with those because many times the creditors and the
bureaus do not have the records correctly reported. In fact, you've come
to the right place, and here's why. Once you have a bankruptcy on your
profile, you cannot afford to have any credit issues until that
bankruptcy is off your  history, as that can prevent you from getting
new credit to reestablish your financial life. What many people don't
notice is that some of the accounts they put into the bankruptcy are not
even listed that way. It then looks like you not only filed bankruptcy
but did not take care of all the problems when doing so and those
accounts look like they are still open and you still owe on them, which
we know is not the case. This helps you because if the accounts are not
correct they must be changed or deleted from the credit report and it
can cause the bankruptcy itself to be challenged. 

**#7- "Should I call all the Collection Agency or Creditors I owe on
here to pay them off before starting?"**
No, we do not have you contact any of these accounts to pay them off
until they have proven they have the legal documents to collect the fees
and this is truly your account. And in that case, we're going to teach
you how to debt negotiate with them so you only pay anywhere from % to %
in most cases and we're going to teach you how to do it properly so they
will never be able to come back on you.

**#8- "My loan officer said I need to be at a 680 credit score (or
whatever credit score number); how long will it take to do that?"**
Well, Federal law mandates that we cannot tell you that, or that by
doing this or that, it will take your score up or down. Only the
 bureaus have the right to raise a consumer's scores up or down.
However, I can tell you there are many things that affect your score,
such as your good, minus your bad, your debt ratios, the types of
accounts you've had, how you've made good, steady payments, how many
inquiries you've had in the last two years, and then how each industry
scores differently that makes up your credit score. For instance, if we
cloned you and took you to a banker, a car dealership, and a mortgage
company and all pulled your credit at the same second, you would get
three different scores because each of those industries looks at
different aspects of those 6 things and scores you differently.

**#9- "How much does it cost?"**
(Monthly) The first work fee of $ is billed after we complete the setup
work, usually within 5 days from the day you begin. The first monthly
fee is $ and will continue on the following month on same day of the
month for services performed the previous month.

(Pay-Per-Day) The first work fee of $ is billed after we complete the
setup work, usually within 5 days from the day you begin. After that,
you only pay for the items that are deleted. Payments of $ towards your
balance are made monthly, starting 30 days from your enrollment date. If
your monthly balance exceeds $, you have the option of receiving a 20%
discount if you pay the balance in full each month  

**#10- "How long does it take?"**
The answer to this is dependent upon each individual client and the work
that needs to be done on your personal credit file. It really depends on
the severity of your credit, the number of items and how far you want to
go with it. Our process is not a miracle cure and does not solve all of
your credit issues overnight. It takes time, persistence and pressure to
show you the best results. Based on our experience, our clients
generally finish our program within 6-8 months, but some clients can
graduate earlier while others may take a little longer. After ? months,
if you're still in the program and you've done everything we've
asked, as well as having no additional negative accounts added to the
report since your start date, your monthly price/per item deletion price
is decreased to $. 

**#11- "How do I get started?"**
Your enrollment can be completed in just a few minutes and I will walk
you through it step by step.

**#12- "How do I track my progress?"**
We give you full transparency. You will receive (3) updated credit
reports in the mail every 30-45 days from the credit bureaus. The credit
reports will each include an "Investigation Results" page that clearly
shows a list of the items investigated along with the result for each
investigation. We ask that you email, fax, or upload the results to us.
We also provide you with a real-time online portal and post the results
for you so you know exactly where you stand.

**#13- "Can you help me build credit?"**
Absolutely! We are experts and will strategically guide you through the
process of building new credit. If you ever want your FICO to get up
there over 720, it is imperative you start with a strong foundation of
revolving and installment trade lines.

#14- "Is credit repair legal?" 
Yes, credit improvement is legal if you follow the guidelines of the
Fair Credit Reporting Act and the Credit Repair Organizations Act.

**#15- "Do you guarantee my score will improve?"**
No one in this business can guarantee that but we are confident that
with time and persistence we are usually able to delete the majority of
harmful inaccurate items affecting your credit. 

**#16- "I have a creditor reporting a debt with an inflated balance, can
you help me?"**
Similar to the audit and verification process, we use our knowledge of
consumer protection laws to dispute negative accounts. In most cases we
not only contact the credit bureaus, we also contact the creditor or
collection agency directly. We place the burden of proof squarely on
their shoulders and are often successful in resolving some of the most
difficult problems quickly and inexpensively.

#17- "Can you delete inquiries?" 
Yes, inquiries are part of your credit history and impact your credit
scores. We can challenge inaccurate, questionable and excessive
inquiries.

**#18- "How do you help with expired debt?"**
Similar to the audit and verification process, we use our knowledge of
consumer protection laws to dispute negative accounts. Expired debt
doesn't mean the debt is no longer owned, it means that you can no
longer be sued for it. Once this is noted, we contact the creditors and
collectors directly and use our knowledge of consumer law to have them
provide validation of the alleged debt they claim you owe. If they
can't prove it they must remove it. 

#19- "I have old collection accounts, should I pay them?"
Sometimes paying off old debts can reduce your credit score. We would
like to review your credit reports and make recommendations based on our
findings. Chances are we may be able to remove some of the outdated
information saving you hundreds or even thousands of dollars. If the
accounts are verified, we will provide advice on helping you settle
those debts. Keep in mind, we are not a debt settlement company and do
not handle any of your settlement funds and we do not physically call
your creditors to negotiate, rather; we will discuss the individual
situation with you and based on our recommendations and your budget, we
can offer suggestions to help you.

**#20- "What is the difference between a debt management company and
your services?"**
Debt Management is often referred to as consumer credit counseling
services, debt consolidation or debt settlement. That is not what we are
about. They work with creditors to negotiate interest, balances or to
negotiate a re-payment plan. A monthly debt payment is established and
the 3rd party handles the disbursement of payments. Essentially, debt
management companies are in charge of your debts. We on the other hand,
are in charge of your credit. We are here to help eliminate inaccurate,
questionable negative information and consult you regarding every aspect
of your credit, however; if you are seeking a debt management program,
we can certainly point you in the right direction. Chances are with a
little bit of good advice you can handle the situation on your own.

**#21- "I'm in a hurry to get a mortgage; can you help me improve my
credit in 30 days?"**
Unfortunately, the answer is NO. We are using consumer protection laws
and drafting verification disputes which take time and often a lot of
persistence to achieve results. Most of our clients see the majority of
their progress within the first 60-90 days, but in some cases it may
take even longer depending on your situation. If you are trying to get a
home loan within the next 30 days, it is unlikely you will see major
changes that are enough to impact the loan you are applying for.

**#22- "Do you dispute with the credit bureaus or do you dispute with
the creditors?"**
Both! Unlike most "credit repair" companies, we go above and beyond by
not only disputing items with the 3 major credit bureaus, we do direct
creditor interventions with creditors and debt collection agencies which
often yields for better results.   

Bruce, I'd love to hear the ways in which you've been able to
benefit from this course content so far and/or how you feel you can use
this content moving forward. Feel free to reach out to me if you have
any questions or to simply share your thoughts, Ah Ha Moments, and
Successes at elle@educreditsolutions.com
