=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-align: center;" class=3D""><=
br style=3D"color: #2a65c5;color: #000000;"></div>=0A<span style=3D"color: =
#000000; font-size: 17px; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-=
decoration: inherit;" class=3D""> <br>Hey there Bruce! You=E2=80=99ve made =
it through 2/3 of the course. We've covered the basics of the FDCPA so that=
 you're equipped with handling collection debt, expired debt and all of tho=
se sneaky little collection agencies and junk debt buyers. We've also went =
over the basics of the FCRA to gain an understanding of what is required of=
 the consumer reporting agencies and data furnishers during the verificatio=
n process. This means you have the tools necessary for Debt Verification an=
d Validation, the basics of the Dispute Process. The remaining part of the =
course will cover dispute flows when dealing with the consumer reporting ag=
encies, creditors, and collectors. But before we get into all of that, let'=
s take a break with come bonus content. In the coming days I advise that yo=
u take the time to go back over the lessons and your notes to=0Aensure that=
 you have an understanding of the material we've covered in Dispute 21 so f=
ar. Understanding the information you've been presented is important to car=
rying out the dispute flow. As the business owner, it's not just enough to =
know the what, you will need to know the why and how in order to be success=
ful in this part of your business. <br><br>Now, on to today's content... <b=
r><br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; text-align: left;=
">Bruce, <span style=3D"color: #000000; font-size: inherit; font-weight: 40=
0; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit; font-family: arial;">Cus=
tomer objections can be difficult to handle, and many sales professionals f=
ear this situation <span style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: 14px; font-wei=
ght: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit;" class=3D"">(=
Yes, I know that you are in credit repair. But, like it or not, as a busine=
ss owner you ARE a sales professional. Or at least you'd want to become=0Ao=
ne if you want to stay in business).</span> However, objections communicate=
 important and valuable information about customer concerns, needs, and fea=
rs. Most objections can be effectively handled with a simple re-framing pro=
cess that puts the issue into perspective.</span><br><br><div style=3D"marg=
in: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: arial; font-=
size: 17px; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: start;">Custo=
mer objections can revolve around an array of issues. They may have concern=
s about the sincerity of the salesperson, the quality of the product, the r=
eliability of service, the price, or the amount of risk involved. </div>=0A=
<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: #222222; fon=
t-family: arial; font-style: normal; text-align: start;"><span style=3D"col=
or: inherit; font-size: inherit; font-weight: bold; line-height: inherit; t=
ext-decoration: underline;" class=3D"">Four Types of Objections </span></di=
v>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: #000000=
; font-family: arial; font-size: 17px; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400=
; text-align: start;">Objections can be generally classified into four type=
s:</div>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-wei=
ght: 400; color: #222222; font-family: arial; font-size: 15px; font-style: =
normal; text-align: start;">1. Price/Risk</div>=0A<div style=3D"margin: 0; =
outline: none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: arial; font-size: 1=
7px; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: start;">Price, cost,=
 budget, or ROI concerns all fall into this category. Price objections are =
often really about risk. If the sales representative has justified the cost=
 by building value during the interaction, the customer will be less worrie=
d.</div>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-wei=
ght: 400; color: #222222; font-family: arial; font-size: 15px; font-style: =
normal; text-align: start;">2. Quality of Service</div>=0A<div style=3D"mar=
gin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: arial; font=
-size: 17px; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: start;">If t=
he customer is concerned about the quality of your products or services =
=E2=80=93 e.g., they express doubts about product quality, the training of =
your personnel, speed or responsiveness of service, or compatibility =
=E2=80=93 these are examples of quality of service concerns.</div>=0A<br><d=
iv style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; font-weight: 400; color: =
#222222; font-family: arial; font-size: 15px; font-style: normal; text-alig=
n: start;">3. Trust/Relationship</div>=0A<div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: =
none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: arial; font-size: 17px; font=
-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: start;">The customer might be=
 concerned with the legitimacy or credibility of you or your company. (This=
 indicates that a good relationship has not been established between the sa=
lesperson and the client.)</div>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: no=
ne; padding: 0; font-weight: 400; color: #222222; font-family: arial; font-=
size: 15px; font-style: normal; text-align: start;">4. Stall</div>=0A<div s=
tyle=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: =
arial; font-size: 17px; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: s=
tart;">Customers sometimes attempt to stall their decision. The closer the =
sale is to closing, the more pressure the customer feels, and if there is a=
ny remaining conflict or anxiety (or no sense of urgency) they may try to s=
tall.</div>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0; color=
: #000000; font-family: arial; font-size: 17px; font-style: normal; font-we=
ight: 400; text-align: start;">If you have anticipated and prepared for the=
 four types of objections, you won=E2=80=99t panic when an objection comes =
up. Remember that the customer is behaving quite normally for anyone about =
to take a risk and make a purchase. The customer is uncertain about making =
a change, and needs reassurance. </div>=0A<br><div style=3D"margin: 0; outl=
ine: none; padding: 0; color: #000000; font-family: arial; font-size: 17px;=
 font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; text-align: start;">The best course =
of action is to direct their focus back to the larger context in which the =
purchase is being made. Below are some rebuttals and answers to what we've =
found to be the most commonly asked credit repair questions.</div>=0A <br><=
